{"subjectId":"sql","id":"group-by","title":"GROUP BY","group":"Functions & Aggregation","order":24,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>GROUP BY</code> splits rows into groups that share the same values, then aggregates each group into a single output row.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>One row per group</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id,\n       COUNT(*)            AS headcount,\n       SUM(salary)         AS payroll,\n       ROUND(AVG(salary))  AS avg_salary,\n       MAX(salary)         AS top_salary\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | headcount | payroll | avg_salary | top_salary\n--------+-----------+---------+------------+-----------\nNULL    | 1         | 60000   | 60000.0    | 60000\n1       | 4         | 460000  | 115000.0   | 150000\n2       | 3         | 252000  | 84000.0    | 110000\n3       | 2         | 165000  | 82500.0    | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Dev, with no department, forms his own <code>NULL</code> group — all <code>NULL</code>s are grouped together.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-rule\"></i>The golden rule</h3>\n<p>Every column in <code>SELECT</code> must either be in <code>GROUP BY</code> or be inside an aggregate. Otherwise the query asks for “the name” of a group that contains four names.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQLite — allowed, but not portable</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id, name, MAX(salary) AS top_salary      -- name is not grouped or aggregated\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | name  | top_salary\n--------+-------+-----------\n1       | Asha  | 150000\n2       | Priya | 110000\n3       | Sara  | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>PostgreSQL, SQL Server and Oracle reject that query (<em>column must appear in the GROUP BY clause or be used in an aggregate function</em>); MySQL rejects it under the default <code>ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY</code>. SQLite accepts it and, as a special case with a single <code>MAX</code>/<code>MIN</code>, returns the name from the max row. Don't rely on it — use a window function or a join (see <em>Top-N per group</em>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-columns\"></i>Grouping by several columns or expressions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT p.category,\n       o.status,\n       SUM(oi.quantity) AS units\nFROM order_items oi\nJOIN orders o   ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\nJOIN products p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\nGROUP BY p.category, o.status\nORDER BY p.category, o.status;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>category    | status    | units\n------------+-----------+------\nElectronics | cancelled | 2\nElectronics | delivered | 3\nElectronics | shipped   | 1\nFurniture   | delivered | 2\nFurniture   | shipped   | 3\nStationery  | delivered | 20\nStationery  | pending   | 20</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT strftime('%Y', hire_date) AS hire_year, COUNT(*) AS hires\nFROM employees\nWHERE hire_date &gt;= '2020-01-01'\nGROUP BY hire_year\nORDER BY hire_year;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hire_year | hires\n----------+------\n2020      | 1\n2021      | 1\n2022      | 1\n2023      | 1\n2024      | 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sum\"></i>Subtotals: ROLLUP, CUBE, GROUPING SETS</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL / SQL Server / Oracle / MySQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id, EXTRACT(YEAR FROM hire_date) AS yr, SUM(salary)\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY ROLLUP (dept_id, yr);          -- per (dept, year), per dept, grand total\n\n-- MySQL: GROUP BY dept_id, yr WITH ROLLUP\n-- GROUPING SETS ((dept_id), (yr), ())  -- exactly the groupings you list\n-- CUBE (dept_id, yr)                   -- every combination</code></pre></figure>\n<p>SQLite has no <code>ROLLUP</code>; the same result is a <code>UNION ALL</code> of the groupings:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT d.name AS department, SUM(e.salary) AS payroll\nFROM employees e JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nGROUP BY d.name\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'ALL DEPARTMENTS', SUM(e.salary)\nFROM employees e JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nORDER BY payroll;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>department      | payroll\n----------------+--------\nHR              | 165000\nSales           | 252000\nEngineering     | 460000\nALL DEPARTMENTS | 877000</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you GROUP BY a column that is not in the SELECT list?</summary><div><p>Yes — <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employees GROUP BY dept_id</code> returns one count per department without showing which is which. It is legal, just rarely useful. The reverse (selecting a non-grouped, non-aggregated column) is the error.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does GROUP BY do with NULL values?</summary><div><p>It treats all <code>NULL</code>s in the grouping column as one group (even though <code>NULL = NULL</code> is not true). The group shows up with <code>NULL</code> as its key.</p></div></details></section>"}